#{[17592186540286 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540316 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540286 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540286 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=5\\cos t\\,i+6\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/07/078f9509a08715c2a842bc9b8e12bfb3da9b9d77950275174ac684b399d06a68.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540286 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a32b1b6-1443-5fe5-830b-fde1a97630b5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540286 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -5\\sin t,6\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}6\\sin t\\cdot(-5\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=30\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=30\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(15-15\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[15t-\\frac{15}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(30 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=30 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=30 \\pi.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540309 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540298 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540298 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/07/078757ceefd30bb15326569c8fdd2f58b92a1a1cf9679c0d1ea662f387cbe553.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$, where\n\n- $C_{1}$ is parametrized by $r_{1}(t) = 7t^{2}i - 3t^{3}j$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1]$, and\n- $C_{2}$ is parametrized by $r_{2}(t) = 7i + 3tj$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1]$.\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "865c8611-6d60-511d-9bf4-cbb5278c6f73" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540298 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=14t\\,i-9{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=3j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot 14t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =42{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =42[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =42\\left(\\frac{1^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-1)^{5}}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =42\\left(\\frac{1}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =42\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540301 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540305 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540301 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540301 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=2\\cos t\\,i+7\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/92/92ce9f04a78465f03756605514aab3c48396c66eb8863f16a64b563a8cace6ee.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540301 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "927aab7c-38ca-5938-9f5e-f646a0bd4394" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540301 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -2\\sin t,7\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}7\\sin t\\cdot(-2\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=14\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=14\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(7-7\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[7t-\\frac{7}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(14 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=14 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=14 \\pi.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540302 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540290 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540302 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186540302 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem 