0962 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560962 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea3760f2-d10c-5617-a3f3-b8335b0f89d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)={e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=y$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f61d70b2-c968-54d9-b087-2ae80ef01b89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561062 #edb/ref 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}-{e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area.  Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561066 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186561091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561066 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0413025a-a6cd-5d63-8b51-14b775eb02ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561066 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186561018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561073 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186561066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561073 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5743bec9-9fc8-52ec-8926-6eb7cdd3fcc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561073 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186561062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561076 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69e681a0-9b8e-5a97-98d5-a5c36baee9a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561076 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186561027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561091 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186561076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561091 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "badd52ef-742f-5ca8-9362-960bb5d0223a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561091 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186560984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561097 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186561073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561097 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dfd8a0a5-40c9-5766-af65-8e056002dbdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561097 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186561063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186561558 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186561531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186561531 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,9 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 3 e^{3 x}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186561531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0cc890ec-db2e-5d10-bd57-1f689423327c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186561531 #edb/ref 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186561533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 175